 --results-found 1 --results-screened 1 --results-admitted 0 --saturation-signal saturated --harm-finding 0 \
 --findings-note "P3a CONFIRMED AND P3b CONFIRMED. HTTP 200, 3.2 MB, 80 pages; the text layer is real (about 93 thousand characters; the twelve low-text pages are stair-inventory plan sheets with riser/tread/width/surface columns, one statistics table, and blank end pages -- the blank one was rendered and looked at). 'ramp' occurs ten times and every one is incidental: the CPSC scope ('stair, ramp and landing accidents'), 'alternatives to stairs ... (elevators, ramps, etc)', a stair 'should be clearly seen as a stair and not mistaken for a ramp', a fall on a stair 'is not like a fall on an even surface like a ramp', and six citations of a forthcoming Templer book (below). 'slope' occurs zero times. 'gradient' occurs eight times and is a FALSE FRIEND: every one is the report's 'orientation gradient', a measure of how many visual orientation factors change from step to step, not an inclination. NO RAMP SLOPE OR LENGTH RECOMMENDATION EXISTS IN THE DOCUMENT. Dated 'February 1976, Issued November 1978' on its title page. ONE FINDING FOR THE FOOTNOTE-c QUESTION, recorded and not resolved: the report repeatedly cites 'Templer, J., Stairs and Ramps, Dowden, Hutchinson and Ross, Stroudsburg, PA., forthcoming' -- a Templer book on stairs AND ramps announced before REF-01005's 1979 report. It joins the 1974 dissertation and the FHWA ramp study (REF-01008) as a possible referent of 'Templer, 1977'; none is chosen." \
