 --redescription "REHOME TO stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility (an ACTIVE slug in the slugs table), NOT ADMITTED ON PARAMETER 3. Retrieved in full from NIST (nbsir78-1554.pdf, persisted) and read -- the text layer is sound, and the low-text pages were checked. It is what its title says and what this row's own staging note predicted: a behavioural and accident study of STAIR users for the Consumer Product Safety Commission, with performance statements on risers, treads, handrails, lighting and visual orientation. It contains NO RAMP SLOPE OR LENGTH RECOMMENDATION: every 'ramp' is incidental (scope, alternatives to stairs, not mistaking a stair for a ramp, falls on stairs versus falls on ramps, and citations), and every 'gradient' is the report's 'orientation gradient', a perceptual measure, not a slope. R15 CORRECTIONS: the staged title gives 1978; the title page reads 'February 1976, Issued November 1978' -- the report was written two years before it was issued. The staging premise (nearest resolvable item to footnote c's 'Templer, 1977') was already retracted on 2026-09-20 in favour of candidate 125, now REF-01008. WHAT THE READ ADDS TO THE FOOTNOTE-c QUESTION, WITHOUT ANSWERING IT: the report cites 'Templer, J., Stairs and Ramps, Dowden, Hutchinson and Ross, Stroudsburg, PA., forthcoming' six times -- a Templer book on ramps announced in a 1976-78 federal report. That is a third possible referent for 'Templer, 1977' beside the 1974 dissertation and the FHWA ramp study, and it is NOT chosen here (candidate 115 stays PENDING). Rehome, not out-of-scope: stair-user behaviour, risers, treads and handrails are that slug's subject, and a later pass there should read it as primary stair evidence." 2>&1 | tail -4
